            {
              "id": "3f785c5e-ea2f-3f0a-bcf2-ee07b36cbb3b",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nIn C++ programming, \"Avoid Using Weak or Non-Cryptographic Random Number Generators\" vulnerability refers to the use of weak or non-cryptographic random number generators in the code. This can lead to predictability in the generated numbers, which can be exploited by attackers to predict the behavior of the program, leading to security vulnerabilities.\n\nWeak or non-cryptographic random number generators do not provide a sufficient level of randomness and can produce predictable outcomes. They are not suitable for generating cryptographic keys or other sensitive data.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, use a strong cryptographic random number generator. In C++, you can use the `<random>` library, which provides a variety of random number generators. For cryptographic purposes, consider using a library specifically designed for cryptography, such as OpenSSL.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is an example of how to use the `<random>` library to generate random numbers in C++:\n\n```cpp\n#include <random>\n\nstd::random_device rd;  // Will be used to obtain a seed for the random number engine\nstd::mt19937 gen(rd()); // Standard mersenne_twister_engine seeded with rd()\nstd::uniform_int_distribution<> distrib(1, 6);\n\nint random_number = distrib(gen);\n```\n\nIn this code, `std::random_device` is a uniformly-distributed integer random number generator that produces non-deterministic random numbers. `std::mt19937` is a Mersenne Twister random number generator, and `std::uniform_int_distribution` is a random number distribution that produces integers according to a uniform discrete distribution.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe above code example requires the `<random>` library.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-338: Use of Cryptographically Weak PRNG](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/338.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS148264"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "36eb4e25-8896-3116-a207-c403d125d87c",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Prohibited C Function Identified\" vulnerability in C++ programming language refers to the use of certain C standard library functions that are considered unsafe due to their potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other security issues. In this case, the `printf` function is used, which can lead to format string vulnerabilities if not used carefully.\n\nFormat string vulnerabilities occur when the format string passed to `printf` (or similar functions) is not a constant, but is instead constructed dynamically or influenced by an attacker. This can allow an attacker to execute arbitrary code, read the stack, or cause a crash.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to avoid using `printf` and similar functions with a non-constant format string. Instead, use safer alternatives that do not have this vulnerability, such as `cout` in C++.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a fixed version of the code:\n\n```cpp\n#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n\nstd::vector<int> samples;\nint trial;\n\n// ...\n\nstd::cout << static_cast<double>(samples[trial]) << \" ms\\n\";\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<iostream>`: for `std::cout`\n- `<vector>`: for `std::vector`\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-134: Use of Externally-Controlled Format String](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/134.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "36521c3e-ad9b-3685-a485-51f1c18cf064",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Prohibited C Function Found\" is a vulnerability that occurs when a C++ program uses a function that is considered unsafe due to its potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other security problems. The `getenv` function is one such function. It returns a pointer to a string that is associated with the matched environment variable name. This can lead to security vulnerabilities if the returned string is modified, or if the program does not handle the possibility that `getenv` could return a null pointer if the specified environment variable does not exist.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, avoid using the `getenv` function. Instead, consider using safer alternatives that are less prone to security issues. Always validate and sanitize input, and handle the possibility of null pointers. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of using `getenv`, you can use a safer alternative like `getenv_s`. Here is an example of how to use it:\n\n```cpp\n#include <stdlib.h>\n\nchar* value;\nsize_t requiredSize;\n\ngetenv_s(&requiredSize, NULL, 0, \"ENV_VAR\");\nif (requiredSize == 0)\n{\n    printf(\"The environment variable does not exist.\\n\");\n}\nelse\n{\n    value = (char*) malloc(requiredSize * sizeof(char));\n    if (value != NULL)\n    {\n        getenv_s(&requiredSize, value, requiredSize, \"ENV_VAR\");\n        printf(\"The value of the environment variable is: %s\\n\", value);\n    }\n    free(value);\n}\n```\n\nIn this code, `getenv_s` is used to safely get the value of an environment variable. It checks if the environment variable exists and handles the possibility of a null pointer.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `stdlib.h` library.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "b4078117-258d-3b52-91ca-8d3e2565f5a6",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strlen` function in C and C++ is used to find the length of a string. However, it can lead to vulnerabilities if not used properly. The function counts the number of characters in a string until it reaches a null character (`'\\0'`). If the string is not null-terminated, `strlen` will continue reading memory until it encounters a null character, potentially leading to buffer overflows, memory corruption, and other undefined behavior.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always ensure that your strings are null-terminated. Be careful when manipulating strings, especially when using functions like `strcpy` or `strcat` that can overwrite the null terminator. Also, consider using safer alternatives to `strlen` that take a maximum length parameter, such as `strnlen`.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of:\n\n```cpp\nFORWARD_PREFIX_LEN = strlen(FORWARD_PREFIX);\n```\n\nYou could use:\n\n```cpp\nFORWARD_PREFIX_LEN = strnlen(FORWARD_PREFIX, MAX_PREFIX_LEN);\n```\n\nWhere `MAX_PREFIX_LEN` is the maximum expected length of `FORWARD_PREFIX`. This ensures that `strnlen` will not read past `MAX_PREFIX_LEN` characters, even if it does not encounter a null character.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `strlen` and `strnlen` functions are part of the C standard library, so no additional libraries are needed.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C/C++ Vulnerabilities](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_Quick_Reference_Guide_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-126: Buffer Over-read](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/126.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS140021"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "1c7ebcac-575f-33ed-86e6-9c5f2bc6f768",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of insecure or unsafe C library functions that can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or integer overflows. In this case, the `printf` function is used, which can lead to format string vulnerabilities if not used properly.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to these functions. For example, instead of using `printf`, you can use `fprintf` or `snprintf` which are safer as they allow you to specify the maximum number of characters to be written to the output.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the code:\n\n```cpp\n#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    char buffer[50];\n    int a = 10;\n    snprintf(buffer, sizeof(buffer), \"%d\", a);\n    printf(\"%s\\n\", buffer);\n    return 0;\n}\n```\n\nIn this code, `snprintf` is used instead of `printf`. This function writes the formatted output to the string `buffer` and ensures that not more than `sizeof(buffer)` characters are written, preventing buffer overflow.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `stdio.h` library to execute properly.\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n\n## Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are valid and active for anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "2e1e9cba-9e14-3be2-b1dc-8979450f12d4",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the analysis identified a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination memory regions in the memory copy operation. The destination and source may refer to overlapping memory, as indicated by the dataflow analysis. This can lead to unpredictable program behavior, data corruption, or crashes. The use of a direct memory copy function without ensuring that the source and destination do not overlap is unsafe. There are no explicit checks or guards in the code to prevent this overlap, and the function verdict confirms the issue as genuine. The source and destination are both non-null, but the overlap risk remains the primary concern.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo prevent undefined behavior when the source and destination memory regions may overlap, use a memory move function that safely handles overlapping regions. Replace the direct memory copy with a function designed for this purpose:\n\n```cpp\nmemmove(buf, v, sizeof(v));\n```\n\nThis change ensures that the operation is safe even if the source and destination overlap, as `memmove` is specifically designed to handle such cases. Always verify that the size argument accurately reflects the intended number of bytes to copy, and that both pointers are valid and properly allocated.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "2c910491-3474-376e-9081-661bc14a6fb3",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Prohibited C Function Found\" is a vulnerability that occurs when a C++ program uses a function that is considered unsafe due to its potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other security problems. The `getenv` function is one such function. It returns a pointer to a string that is associated with the matched environment variable name. This can lead to security vulnerabilities if the returned string is modified, or if the program does not handle the possibility that `getenv` could return a null pointer if the specified environment variable does not exist.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, avoid using the `getenv` function. Instead, consider using safer alternatives that are less prone to security issues. Always validate and sanitize input, and handle the possibility of null pointers. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of using `getenv`, you can use a safer alternative like `getenv_s`. Here is an example of how to use it:\n\n```cpp\n#include <stdlib.h>\n\nchar* value;\nsize_t requiredSize;\n\ngetenv_s(&requiredSize, NULL, 0, \"ENV_VAR\");\nif (requiredSize == 0)\n{\n    printf(\"The environment variable does not exist.\\n\");\n}\nelse\n{\n    value = (char*) malloc(requiredSize * sizeof(char));\n    if (value != NULL)\n    {\n        getenv_s(&requiredSize, value, requiredSize, \"ENV_VAR\");\n        printf(\"The value of the environment variable is: %s\\n\", value);\n    }\n    free(value);\n}\n```\n\nIn this code, `getenv_s` is used to safely get the value of an environment variable. It checks if the environment variable exists and handles the possibility of a null pointer.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `stdlib.h` library.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "9688fb72-879e-377f-adbe-4d8219287877",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strlen` function in C++ is used to find the length of a string. However, it can lead to vulnerabilities if not used properly. The function counts the number of characters in a string up to but not including the first null character. If the string is not null-terminated, `strlen` will continue reading memory until it encounters a null character, which can lead to buffer overflows, memory corruption, and other undefined behavior.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always ensure that your strings are null-terminated. This can be done by initializing your strings with a null character (`'\\0'`) at the end, or by using functions that automatically null-terminate the string for you, such as `strncpy`.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of using `strlen`, consider using `strnlen`, which takes an additional parameter specifying the maximum length of the string. This can prevent buffer overflows by ensuring that `strnlen` does not read past the end of the buffer.\n\nHere is how you can modify the code:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstring> // for strnlen\n\n// ...\n\nconst size_t max_length = 100; // replace with the actual maximum length\ndetail::widen_impl(str, std::strnlen(str, max_length));\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library:\n\n- `<cstring>`: This library provides functions to manipulate C strings and arrays.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-126: Buffer Over-read](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/126.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS140021"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "913104a6-c0e2-32f2-ba50-ceb365778aa1",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Prohibited C Function Found\" is a vulnerability that occurs when a C++ program uses a function that is considered unsafe due to its potential to cause buffer overflow, format string vulnerabilities, integer overflow, etc. These functions are often prohibited because they do not perform bounds checking or input validation, which can lead to serious security issues.\n\nIn the provided code, `std::wcslen(str)` is a function that can lead to buffer overflow if the string `str` is not null-terminated. This function calculates the length of a wide string until it encounters a null character. If a null character is not found, the function may continue reading beyond the memory allocated for the string, leading to a buffer overflow.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives that perform bounds checking or input validation. In this case, you can use `std::wstring` and its `length()` or `size()` member functions to get the length of the string.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `std::wcslen(str)` function with `std::wstring::length()` or `std::wstring::size()` as shown below:\n\n```cpp\nstd::wstring wstr = str;\ndetail::narrow_impl(wstr, wstr.length());\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<string>`: This is required for `std::wstring`.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-242: Use of Inherently Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/242.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "79c0374a-e9fe-3b20-aea9-9922553709f6",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ programming language refers to the use of certain C standard library functions that are considered unsafe due to their potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other issues that can lead to arbitrary code execution or denial of service. One such function is `mbsrtowcs`.\n\nThe `mbsrtowcs` function is used to convert a multibyte string to a wide character string. However, it does not perform any bounds checking on the destination buffer, which can lead to buffer overflow if the source string is too large.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, avoid using the `mbsrtowcs` function. Instead, use safer alternatives that perform bounds checking, such as `mbsrtowcs_s` (in Microsoft's C library) or `mbsnrtowcs` (in GNU's C library).\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `mbsrtowcs` function with `mbsnrtowcs` or `mbsrtowcs_s` and ensure to provide the size of the destination buffer as an argument to these functions.\n\n```cpp\n// Unsafe code\nchar* src = /* source multibyte string */;\nwchar_t dest[100];\nmbsrtowcs(dest, &src, 100, NULL);\n\n// Safe code (GNU)\nchar* src = /* source multibyte string */;\nwchar_t dest[100];\nmbsnrtowcs(dest, &src, strlen(src), 100, NULL);\n\n// Safe code (Microsoft)\nchar* src = /* source multibyte string */;\nwchar_t dest[100];\nsize_t converted;\nmbsrtowcs_s(&converted, dest, 100, &src, _TRUNCATE, NULL);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the C standard library (`<cstdlib>` in C++, `<stdlib.h>` in C) for the `mbsrtowcs`, `mbsnrtowcs`, and `mbsrtowcs_s` functions, and the string library (`<cstring>` in C++, `<string.h>` in C) for the `strlen` function.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "57655b9f-1235-3082-9980-3e5fe259aea3",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ programming language refers to the use of certain C standard library functions that are considered unsafe due to their potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other issues that can lead to arbitrary code execution or denial of service. One such function is `mbsrtowcs`.\n\nThe `mbsrtowcs` function is used to convert a multibyte string to a wide character string. However, it does not perform any bounds checking on the destination buffer, which can lead to buffer overflow if the source string is too large.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, avoid using the `mbsrtowcs` function. Instead, use safer alternatives that perform bounds checking, such as `mbsrtowcs_s` (in Microsoft's C library) or `mbsnrtowcs` (in GNU's C library).\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `mbsrtowcs` function with `mbsnrtowcs` or `mbsrtowcs_s` and ensure to provide the size of the destination buffer as an argument to these functions.\n\n```cpp\n// Unsafe code\nchar* src = /* source multibyte string */;\nwchar_t dest[100];\nmbsrtowcs(dest, &src, 100, NULL);\n\n// Safe code (GNU)\nchar* src = /* source multibyte string */;\nwchar_t dest[100];\nmbsnrtowcs(dest, &src, strlen(src), 100, NULL);\n\n// Safe code (Microsoft)\nchar* src = /* source multibyte string */;\nwchar_t dest[100];\nsize_t converted;\nmbsrtowcs_s(&converted, dest, 100, &src, _TRUNCATE, NULL);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the C standard library (`<cstdlib>` in C++, `<stdlib.h>` in C) for the `mbsrtowcs`, `mbsnrtowcs`, and `mbsrtowcs_s` functions, and the string library (`<cstring>` in C++, `<string.h>` in C) for the `strlen` function.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
