            {
              "id": "ea6e10f4-2902-3ca6-b4a5-2fb60f6259a3",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of C standard library functions that are prone to errors, which can lead to serious security vulnerabilities such as buffer overflows, integer overflows, etc. In the provided code snippet, the function `std::atoi` is used, which is a prohibited C function because it does not perform any error checking. If the input string cannot be converted into an integer, it returns zero, which can lead to unexpected behavior.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using C standard library functions that do not perform error checking. Instead, use safer alternatives provided by the C++ standard library. In this case, you can use `std::stoi` function which throws an exception if the conversion fails.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace `std::atoi` with `std::stoi` as shown below:\n\n```cpp\ntry {\n    min_wgs = min_wgs_str.empty() ? 64 : std::stoi(min_wgs_str);\n} catch (const std::invalid_argument& ia) {\n    // Handle the exception\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<string>`: for `std::string` and `std::stoi`\n- `<stdexcept>`: for `std::invalid_argument`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
